>

  <div class="hero">
    <div class="category">Memory &bull; ESP32</div>
    <h1>ESP32 Board Health<br>Lifetime Tracking With NVS</h1>
    <div class="meta">
      <div class="meta-item"><strong>Board:</strong> Any ESP32</div>
      <div class="meta-item"><strong>Topic:</strong> NVS, Preferences, board history</div>
    </div>
    <div class="series-step">ESP32 Memory Series - Step 6 of 9</div>
    <div class="badge badge-untested">&#9888; Untested</div>
  </div>

  <div class="terminal-note">
    Part of the <a href="/guides/esp32-memory-intro" class="term-link">ESP32 Memory series &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <!-- The Idea -->
  <div class="section" id="the-idea">
    <h2>The Idea</h2>
    <p>Every ESP32 you own has a history. How many times it has booted, how many projects it has run, how long it has been running in total. By default none of that is recorded anywhere. When you reflash a board it is as if its past never happened.</p>
    <p>This guide shows you how to change that. A dedicated NVS namespace called <code>board</code> that you never clear. Every sketch you flash writes to it on boot. Over time it accumulates:</p>
    <ul class="flag-list">
      <li>A total boot count across all projects ever flashed</li>
      <li>The name of the current sketch</li>
      <li>Accumulated uptime across all sessions</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Pull up the serial monitor on any board and immediately know its history.</p>
    <div class="callout callout-why">
      <strong>Why this is useful:</strong>
      When something starts behaving strangely you have context. A board with ten thousand boots and two years of uptime is a different suspect to one with fifty boots and a week of use.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- The Sketch -->
  <div class="section" id="the-sketch">
    <h2>The Sketch</h2>
    <p>Add this to any project. Change the <code>PROJECT_NAME</code> string to match your sketch and the rest looks after itself.</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>{`#include <Preferences.h>

#define PROJECT_NAME "my-project-name"

Preferences boardHealth;

void logBoardHealth() {
    boardHealth.begin("board", false);

    uint32_t totalBoots = boardHealth.getUInt("totalBoots", 0);
    totalBoots++;
    boardHealth.putUInt("totalBoots", totalBoots);

    boardHealth.putString("lastProject", PROJECT_NAME);

    uint32_t totalUptime = boardHealth.getUInt("totalUptime", 0);

    Serial.println("=== Board Health ===");
    Serial.printf("Total boots:     %lu\\n", (unsigned long)totalBoots);
    Serial.printf("Last project:    %s\\n", PROJECT_NAME);
    Serial.printf("Total uptime:    %lu seconds\\n", (unsigned long)totalUptime);
    Serial.println("===================");

    boardHealth.end();
}

void saveUptime() {
    boardHealth.begin("board", false);
    uint32_t totalUptime = boardHealth.getUInt("totalUptime", 0);
    totalUptime += millis() / 1000;
    boardHealth.putUInt("totalUptime", totalUptime);
    boardHealth.end();
}

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(115200);
    delay(1000);
    logBoardHealth();
}

void loop() {
    // Save uptime every 60 seconds
    static uint32_t t0 = 0;
    if (millis() - t0 > 60000) {
        t0 = millis();
        saveUptime();
    }
}`}</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- What You Get -->
  <div class="section" id="what-you-get">
    <h2>What You Get</h2>
    <p>Every board you own builds its own record. A board you have had for two years and run twenty projects on will tell you that. A board you just pulled from a bag will show zero boots and no project history.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Reading Without Reflashing -->
  <div class="section" id="reading-without-reflash">
    <h2>Reading Without Reflashing</h2>
    <p>You can read the board health partition from any sketch without disturbing it. Flash this to any board that has been running the health tracker and get its full history immediately.</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>{`#include <Preferences.h>
Preferences boardHealth;

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(115200);
    delay(1000);

    // Open in read-only mode
    boardHealth.begin("board", true);

    Serial.println("=== Board Health ===");
    Serial.printf("Total boots:  %lu\\n", (unsigned long)boardHealth.getUInt("totalBoots", 0));
    Serial.printf("Last project: %s\\n", boardHealth.getString("lastProject", "none").c_str());
    Serial.printf("Total uptime: %lu seconds\\n", (unsigned long)boardHealth.getUInt("totalUptime", 0));
    Serial.println("===================");

    boardHealth.end();
}

void loop() {}`}</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Notes -->
  <div class="section" id="notes">
    <h2>Notes</h2>
    <h3>The one thing that resets it</h3>
    <p>NVS persists across reflashing but not across a full flash erase. If you use Tools in the Arduino IDE to erase all flash, or use esptool to wipe the chip, the board health partition goes with it. That is the only way to reset it to zero, which means it only happens when you choose it to.</p>
    <h3>Write cycles</h3>
    <p>NVS uses wear levelling to spread writes across the flash cells. Saving uptime every 60 seconds is not going to wear out your flash in any realistic timeframe. If you are concerned, increase the save interval.</p>
  </div>

